\section*{Abstract}
The Proton--Laser Interaction \& Cosmic Data Integration Engine (v1.2) is a high-energy plasma simulation framework integrating astrophysical dataset ingestion, proton--laser acceleration physics, particle-in-cell (PIC) solvers, magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) coupling, GPU-accelerated computation, and encrypted scientific workflows. The engine models proton acceleration mechanisms such as target-normal sheath acceleration (TNSA) and radiation-pressure acceleration (RPA), simulates plasma evolution under Vlasov--Maxwell dynamics, and incorporates cosmic boundary conditions derived from HDF5, FITS, and GEANT4 datasets. This white sheet presents the architecture, physics foundations, computational methodology, and stability governance underlying the engine.

\section{System Overview}
The engine is composed of five principal subsystems:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Cosmic Data Ingestion \& Preprocessing
    \item Proton--Laser Interaction Physics
    \item PIC/MHD Hybrid Solver
    \item Encryption \& Security Subsystem
    \item HPC Infrastructure \& GPU Acceleration
\end{enumerate}
These subsystems operate under encrypted, topology-consistent scientific computation.

\section{Cosmic Data Ingestion \& Preprocessing}
\subsection{Supported Data Formats}
The ingestion layer supports:
\begin{itemize}
    \item HDF5 astrophysics datasets
    \item FITS telescope data
    \item GEANT4 particle-interaction outputs
    \item Cosmic ray spectra
    \item Star-formation models
    \item Interstellar medium (ISM) density maps
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Preprocessing Pipeline}
The pipeline performs coordinate normalization, spectral calibration, cosmic boundary-condition extraction, ISM density field mapping, and proton--laser configuration loading. Laser configuration parameters include pulse duration (fs), intensity (W/cm$^2$), wavelength ($\mu$m), and focal spot size ($\mu$m). This subsystem constructs the cosmic--plasma initial state.

\section{Proton--Laser Interaction Physics}
\subsection{Interaction Zone}
The engine models ultra-intense laser beam propagation, proton bunch initialization, plasma sheath formation, and TNSA/RPA acceleration mechanisms.

\subsection{Governing Equations}
Proton dynamics follow
\[
\frac{dp}{dt} = q\left(E + v \times B\right).
\]
Laser--plasma coupling uses Maxwell's equations:
\[
\nabla \times E = -\frac{\partial B}{\partial t}, \quad
\nabla \times B = \mu_0 J + \mu_0 \epsilon_0 \frac{\partial E}{\partial t}.
\]

\subsection{Plasma Density Evolution}
Plasma density evolution is represented abstractly as
\[
\Delta n = \Psi(p, E, B),
\]
where $\Psi$ encodes the coupling between particle momentum, electric field, and magnetic field. This subsystem defines the high-energy proton acceleration physics.

\section{PIC/MHD Hybrid Solver}
\subsection{PIC\_SOLVER C++ Module}
The PIC\_SOLVER module includes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Vlasov--Maxwell solver
    \item Particle pusher (Boris/Vay)
    \item Field interpolator (Esirkepov/Yee)
    \item Collision operator
    \item MPI/OpenMP scheduler
\end{itemize}

\subsection{MHD Coupling}
MHD coupling models magnetic field evolution, plasma--field feedback, shock formation, and turbulence dynamics, providing a macroscopic description consistent with the microscopic PIC evolution.

\subsection{GPU Acceleration}
GPU acceleration is provided via CUDA/HIP kernels, multi-GPU scaling, and memory-optimized field solvers, enabling high-fidelity plasma evolution at petascale performance.

\section{Encryption \& Security Subsystem}
\subsection{Encryption}
The engine applies AES-256 data-at-rest encryption and TLS 1.3 data-in-transit encryption, with optional integration to hardware security modules (HSMs).

\subsection{Access Control}
Access control is enforced via role-based access control (RBAC) and audit logging, ensuring traceability of scientific workflows.

\subsection{Integrity Verification}
Integrity is verified using Merkle tree hashing and zero-trust compliance, ensuring that simulation state transitions remain tamper-evident and cryptographically validated.

\section{HPC Infrastructure Layer}
\subsection{Compute Resources}
The infrastructure layer includes CPU/GPU nodes, Lustre/GPUdirect storage, and InfiniBand interconnects.

\subsection{Workflow Management}
Workflow management is handled via SLURM/PBS job orchestration, real-time data streams, and cluster-wide monitoring, providing petascale computational throughput for large-scale simulations.

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Data Preparation}
Cosmic datasets are normalized, calibrated, and converted into solver-ready fields, including ISM density maps and cosmic ray spectra.

\subsection{Proton--Laser Initialization}
Laser parameters and proton bunch configurations are loaded into the interaction zone, defining the initial conditions for proton acceleration.

\subsection{PIC/MHD Evolution}
Plasma states are evolved using encrypted Vlasov--Maxwell PIC loops with MHD coupling, capturing both microscopic particle dynamics and macroscopic field behavior.

\subsection{Encryption Enforcement}
All state transitions are encrypted and validated using Merkle-based integrity signatures, ensuring secure and verifiable scientific computation.

\subsection{HPC Execution}
Simulations are executed across multi-GPU nodes with memory-optimized solvers, leveraging parallelism for high-resolution plasma modeling.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Proton Acceleration}
Simulations demonstrate stable TNSA and RPA acceleration profiles, with physically plausible proton energy spectra.

\subsection{Plasma Sheath Formation}
Sheath fields form consistently across cosmic boundary conditions, supporting the expected laser-driven acceleration mechanisms.

\subsection{Magnetic Field Coupling}
MHD coupling produces anisotropic plasma evolution and shock structures, consistent with high-energy plasma behavior.

\subsection{Integrity Stability}
Encrypted computation preserves state integrity across all runs, with successful validation of Merkle tree signatures.

\section{Validation Framework}
\subsection{Scenario Types}
Validation scenarios include:
\begin{itemize}
